module v2.std.layer_import_scan

import v2.std.collection { List }
import v2.std.layer { LayerImportFact }
import v2.std.text { String }

// Transitional host producer (layering-imports-reference-repoint-design.md §3.1): import-syntax
// rows for import-bearing files in the scan roots, plus
// reference_edges_as_import_facts(strict=true) for import-less files; import-syntax wins on (path,
// target_module) collision. Terminal step (namespace import-grammar deletion, Phase 3) deletes the
// import-syntax arm — only then does layer_import_facts_live become single-authority. Consumers:
// v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment, v2.lens.meta_exec_confinement (re-homed here from the
// deleted direction lens v2.lens.layering_imports, 2026-07-25).

// 🟡 dissolve-on: host layer_import_facts stamps LayerImportFact.layer via
// CLI_RUN_LAYER_PREFIX_FROM_DOTTED_MODULE_SCAFFOLD_MARKER — a Rust mirror of
// v2.std.cross_tree.resolution.layer_prefix_from_dotted_qualified_name. Deletes when the builtin
// routes layer through that .dag authority (or a shared host projection beside
// reference_resolution_facts).
// THE LIVE IMPORT-FACT SCAN, SEGREGATED FROM THE LAYER VOCABULARY IT PRODUCES.
//
// layer_import_facts_live is a host seam: its whole body is a self-call answered by the
// v1 interpreter builtin layer_import_facts, which walks real source roots on disk. It
// has no body in the substrate and cannot acquire one, because the answer is a fact about
// a filesystem rather than about its arguments.
//
// It used to live in v2.std.layer beside LayerPrefix and LayerImportFact, which the v2
// compiler's import closure carries for the vocabulary alone -- no compile path calls the
// scan. Emission is decided at MODULE grain, so the seam was emitted into the compiler's
// own Rust crate as panic!("unrealized host seam ..."), a declaration with no behaviour on
// that target sitting in a shipped compiler purely because its neighbours were needed.
// Splitting the producer from the vocabulary drops it out of the closure by identity while
// leaving both real consumers -- the two containment lenses -- reading exactly what they
// read before.
//
// The split is also the standing wall: v2.std.layer is importable by anything, this module
// is imported only by lenses and their witnesses, so a compile-closure declaration that
// starts scanning the tree drags THIS module in and the seam reappears in the emitted
// crate at the same grep the closure census reads.
//
// IT LIVES IN std/ AND NOT IN lens/, and that is a correction rather than a preference. It
// was first homed at v2.lens.layer_import_scan because its only consumers are two lenses,
// which put a NON-LENS module inside the population v2.lens.enforcement.lens_module_gate
// reads as the lens registry's subject -- so the live gate refused it as an unenrolled lens,
// correctly. A producer consumed by lenses is not itself a lens; closure membership is
// decided by REFERENCE and not by directory (DESIGN section 3: paths are discriminators, not
// gospel), so the seam stays out of the compile closure from either home and only the lens
// registry's population is sensitive to which one.
fn layer_import_facts_live(
  std_roots: List<String>,
  extdeps_roots: List<String>
) -> List<LayerImportFact> {
  layer_import_facts(std_roots: std_roots, extdeps_roots: extdeps_roots)
}
